The parse threads and the checker threads started together

A host that starts a thread by forking the thread that starts it (garnet) keeps that thread for most
of a millisecond a fork: ten parse threads were started over 8 ms in which the main thread queued
nothing, and ten checkers over 5 ms more. `startThreads` takes a `together` function: where it is
given, `spawn` makes threads that are not started, and the session that made them all hands them to
it at once (garnet's `Worker.startTogether`: one fork of the caller, which forks them). A chunk posted
to a thread that is not started yet reaches it once it runs. A checker thread that replaces one at
its file limit is started alone.

The TypeScript 6.0.3 compiler project, type check only, a plain build, 15 alternating runs,
milliseconds from the executable's load (minimum / median): the root files queued 13.8 / 14.2 ->
8.6 / 8.9, the other files parsed 40.0 / 44.0 -> 35.2 / 38.5, the checkers started 44.3 / 48.6 ->
39.4 / 42.8, exit 235.7 / 245.2 -> 233.8 / 239.7. What the compiler reports is unchanged.

